01. VPC components

Memorize this:

VPC
 |
 +-- CIDR
 |
 +-- Subnets
 |     |
 |     +-- Public
 |     +-- Private
 |
 +-- Route Tables
 |
 +-- Internet Gateway
 |
 +-- NAT Gateway
 |
 +-- Security Groups
 |
 +-- Network ACLs
 |
 +-- VPC Endpoints
 |
 +-- DNS
 |
 +-- VPC Peering
 |
 +-- Transit Gateway


02. Public subnet traffic:

EC2
 |
 v
Route Table
 |
 | 0.0.0.0/0
 v
Internet Gateway
 |
 v
Internet



03. Private subnet traffic:
EC2
 |
 v
Route Table
 |
 | 0.0.0.0/0
 v
NAT Gateway
 |
 v
Internet Gateway
 |
 v
Internet



04. Private subnet to S3

With an appropriate S3 VPC endpoint:

Private EC2
     |
     v
S3 VPC Endpoint
     |
     v
S3

This can avoid using a NAT Gateway for that S3 traffic.



05. Security Group architecture

A very common production pattern:

                 Internet
                    |
                    v
                 ALB-SG
                    |
                    v
                 APP-SG
                    |
                    v
                  DB-SG

Rules:

ALB-SG
443  <-- Internet

APP-SG
8080 <-- ALB-SG

DB-SG
5432 <-- APP-SG

This is much better than opening everything to:

0.0.0.0/0




Hands-on project:
Don't just memorize VPC.

Build this:

                 Internet
                    |
                    v
                 Route53
                    |
                    v
                   ALB
                /       \
               /         \
         Public AZ-1   Public AZ-2
              |             |
          NAT Gateway   NAT Gateway
              |             |
              v             v
       Private App AZ-1  Private App AZ-2
              |             |
              +------+------+
                     |
                     v
                    RDS

Then deploy:

Terraform
    |
    v
VPC
 |
 +-- 2 Public Subnets
 +-- 2 Private App Subnets
 +-- 2 Private DB Subnets
 +-- IGW
 +-- NAT Gateways
 +-- Route Tables
 +-- Security Groups
 +-- ALB
 +-- EC2
 +-- RDS

Then later replace:

EC2

with:

EKS

and you'll start understanding real AWS DevOps networking rather than just memorizing definitions.
